  "worked_solution": "The angle ${75}^{\u2218}$ is not a special angle. However, we can rewrite it as the sum of two special angles, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{75}^{\u2218}={45}^{\u2218}+{30}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x+y)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $x={45}^{\u2218}$ and $y={30}^{\u2218},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({75}^{\u2218}) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({45}^{\u2218}+{30}^{\u2218}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061{45}^{\u2218}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{30}^{\u2218}+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{45}^{\u2218}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061{30}^{\u2218} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
